 (nasty remarks) are more severe than C's communication difficulties, making C still preferable. Or we could argue that B's reliability and stress handling might outweigh C's egocentric attitude. Need to decide.\n\nGiven safety-critical, crew cooperation is vital. Nasty remarks could be dangerous. C's egocentric and communication difficulty also problematic but perhaps less severe? We need to argue.\n\nWe can say: I still recommend C, because despite B's strong reliability, his nasty remarks pose a higher risk to crew cooperation than C's communication issues, and C's decision quality and calmness are critical.\n\nThus we keep vote for C.\n\nWe need to reference the new fact: B is very conscientious (operational reliability). Also reference earlier facts: B makes nasty remarks, weak memory for numbers. C can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm, understands technology. So we argue.\n\nProceed with public message."}
